WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.692
Back to the theatre's box office.

2
00:00:04.692 --> 00:00:10.727
The city's booking service runs in another building, and the box office reaches it over the network.

3
00:00:10.727 --> 00:00:13.323
Most calls come back in a few milliseconds.

4
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:15.530
Now and then, one gets no answer.

5
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.764
The call times out, and the customer at the counter waits for nothing.

6
00:00:19.764 --> 00:00:24.967
So the theatre wants every booking call timed, and a call that timed out, tried again.

7
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.854
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:28.854 --> 00:00:33.593
In many frameworks, you write @Retry above a method, and it just retries.

9
00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:34.892
Nobody wrote a loop.

10
00:00:34.892 --> 00:00:38.139
So where does the code that retries actually live?

11
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.777
Let's first write it the honest way, inside the method.

12
00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:43.680
Note the time.

13
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.450
Try the booking service, up to three times.

14
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.415
And whatever happens, record how long it took.

15
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:51.249
Now find the real work.

16
00:00:51.249 --> 00:00:53.709
It's one line: the call to reserve.

17
00:00:53.709 --> 00:00:55.676
Everything else is plumbing.

18
00:00:55.676 --> 00:00:58.065
Kotlin is the same, line for line.

19
00:00:58.967 --> 00:01:05.316
And this plumbing gets copied into confirm, into cancel, and into every method it will ever grow.

20
00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:08.196
One line of work, a dozen lines guarding it.

21
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:14.803
Wouldn't it be nice to write the plumbing once, for any method, and keep reserve down to its one line?

22
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.034
Remember the stand-in at the stage door, from the Class loading track?

23
00:01:20.034 --> 00:01:23.636
Every request meant for the actor reaches the stand-in first.

24
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:26.139
Now give the door a row of helpers.

25
00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:27.827
The first is a timekeeper.

26
00:01:27.827 --> 00:01:33.541
It notes the time, says pass it on, and notes the time again when the answer comes back.

27
00:01:33.541 --> 00:01:35.164
The next one tries again.

28
00:01:35.164 --> 00:01:39.644
It says pass it on, and if the answer is a timeout, it says it again.

29
00:01:39.644 --> 00:01:43.085
After the last helper, the request reaches the actor.

30
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.084
Let's map that.

31
00:01:45.084 --> 00:01:49.611
Each helper is an interceptor: plumbing written once, for any method.

32
00:01:49.611 --> 00:01:52.104
Pass it on is a method called proceed.

33
00:01:52.104 --> 00:01:54.072
The row of helpers is a chain.

34
00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:57.680
And the actor is the real method, the one line of work.

35
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.836
Each helper is handed a small note, an Invocation.

36
00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:05.525
It carries the method's name, its arguments, and proceed.

37
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:07.985
Here is the lab's timer.

38
00:02:07.985 --> 00:02:11.024
It notes the start time, and assumes the worst.

39
00:02:11.024 --> 00:02:12.124
It calls proceed.

40
00:02:12.124 --> 00:02:14.840
If nothing was thrown, it marks a success.

41
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.077
And in finally, whatever happened, it records the name, the time and the outcome.

42
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.436
That finally is the tricky line.

43
00:02:22.436 --> 00:02:26.776
It runs on success and on failure, so a failed call is timed too.

44
00:02:26.776 --> 00:02:28.512
Kotlin has the same shape.

45
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:36.331
The helper that tries again is just a loop: after a failure it was told to retry, it calls proceed once more.

46
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.102
Now, does the order of the helpers matter?

47
00:02:40.102 --> 00:02:44.154
The demo sends one booking call that fails twice, then answers.

48
00:02:44.154 --> 00:02:48.014
Once with the timekeeper outside the retry, and once inside.

49
00:02:48.014 --> 00:02:51.359
How many entries does the timekeeper write each way?

50
00:02:51.359 --> 00:02:52.388
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:59.138
Timekeeper outside: one entry, a success.

52
00:02:59.138 --> 00:03:02.789
It saw one call, as long as all the attempts together.

53
00:03:02.789 --> 00:03:05.019
Timekeeper inside: three entries.

54
00:03:05.019 --> 00:03:07.115
Failed, failed, then a success.

55
00:03:07.115 --> 00:03:09.413
It timed every attempt on its own.

56
00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:12.473
Neither is wrong; they measure different things.

57
00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:14.307
The lab puts the timer outside.

58
00:03:14.307 --> 00:03:18.447
The customer at the counter doesn't care that attempt three was quick.

59
00:03:18.447 --> 00:03:19.985
They waited for all three.

60
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.550
How does the container put the chain in front of a real part?

61
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.927
It depends on the part.

62
00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:32.541
If the part has an interface, the JDK writes a stand-in that implements it, a JDK proxy.

63
00:03:32.541 --> 00:03:37.347
Every call runs the chain, then reaches the real object behind it.

64
00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:48.475
If the part has no interface, Byte Buddy, a library that writes classes while the program runs, sews a costume copy: a subclass whose overrides run the chain.

65
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.111
That's where Kotlin bites.

66
00:03:51.111 --> 00:03:55.980
A Kotlin class is final unless you write open, so no subclass may copy it.

67
00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.270
Think of a costume with a do not copy tag sewn in.

68
00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.296
The container checks at start-up, and refuses.

69
00:04:02.296 --> 00:04:06.244
An annotation only works if a stand-in can reach the method.

70
00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:09.365
Now the guardrail: what you retry.

71
00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.499
Selling a ticket takes two calls.

72
00:04:11.499 --> 00:04:15.703
Reserve holds the seat, and confirm turns the hold into a ticket.

73
00:04:15.703 --> 00:04:17.772
Say the first confirm times out.

74
00:04:17.772 --> 00:04:22.428
If the retry wraps both calls as one, what does the booking service see?

75
00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.463
Pause and guess.

76
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:31.336
Reserve, the confirm that timed out, then reserve again, and confirm.

77
00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:36.760
One customer, two seats held, and an empty one on the night that nobody else can buy.

78
00:04:36.967 --> 00:04:41.131
Through the container's proxy, each inner call keeps its own retry.

79
00:04:41.131 --> 00:04:46.289
So the booking service sees reserve, the confirm that timed out, and confirm again.

80
00:04:46.289 --> 00:04:48.464
Only the confirmation was repeated.

81
00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:52.069
Even then, a timeout doesn't say the confirm failed.

82
00:04:52.069 --> 00:04:53.574
It says you don't know.

83
00:04:53.574 --> 00:05:01.231
So retry confirm only if it carries a booking reference, the same on every attempt, so the service can spot a repeat.

84
00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:06.317
And here the stand-in picture breaks.

85
00:05:06.317 --> 00:05:11.663
A real stand-in would notice the same customer coming back twice for the same seat.

86
00:05:11.663 --> 00:05:13.660
An interceptor notices nothing.

87
00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.976
It does exactly what the annotation says, as many times as it says.

88
00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.754
So, where does the retry live?

89
00:05:20.754 --> 00:05:22.238
In a helper at the door.

90
00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:25.638
It runs around the call, and proceed hands the call on.

91
00:05:25.638 --> 00:05:33.241
Write the plumbing once, line the helpers up in the order you mean, and retry only the smallest call that's safe to repeat.

92
00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:34.279
Quick check.

93
00:05:34.279 --> 00:05:40.101
A third helper writes down every request it passes on, and it stands inside the retry.

94
00:05:40.101 --> 00:05:42.944
A cancellation times out once, then works.

95
00:05:42.944 --> 00:05:44.907
How many lines does it write?

96
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:48.411
Two.

97
00:05:48.411 --> 00:05:51.525
Inside the retry, it sees every attempt.

98
00:05:51.525 --> 00:05:54.093
Outside, it would write just one.
